00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
joining us from the government this  morning the technology minister chris  philp minister great to have you on the  program this morning but clearly these  are these are very concerning times  given that the troop build up around  ukraine uh president biden has warned  ukraine's president to prepare for  impact are we really that close to  conflict in your opinion  well we're deeply concerned the buildup  of russian troops around the ukrainian  border has reached unprecedented levels  military units from the far east of  russia have moved into that region  something that hasn't happened for many  many years previously  so i think it is a very serious  situation i think president biden and  our prime minister are right to warn  president putin that the consequences if  they do  invade ukraine will be very very serious  indeed for russia in terms of sanctions  the americans made clear yesterday that  the nordstrom 2 pipe  would not happen if they invade uh in  the united kingdom we're doing  everything we can to support the  ukrainians including supplying uh  military equipment that would help them  and training as well that goes with that  that would help them prevent tanks  russian tanks from entering ukrainian  territory so i think we are urging uh  russia to  get to the table uh to discuss  issues uh that are relevant and to  de-escalate this situation which will  ultimately de-escalation is clearly in  the interests of all concerned uh it is  not too late to de-escalate and that is  what now needs to happen  yeah we will certainly see over there in  the coming days and weeks that the tone  struck by vladimir putin and i do  however want to move on to more domestic  matters minister uh given we have you on  the program this morning  and national insurance and in various  interviews this week ministers from the  cabinet down have all insisted that that  planned increase come april is going to  go ahead yesterday the prime minister  didn't confirm it i'm just wondering  what is your understanding of the  situation right now is that national  national insurance rise in april set in  stone  uh yes it is going ahead it was approved  by the whole cabinet it was passed by  parliament  with a significant  majority and the money is needed to fund  the nhs which is something i think that  is a national priority it's 36 billion  pounds over three years to fund the nhs  and social care  we need to put that money in to make  sure the nhs has the resources it needs  to recover after the pandemic and this  is a  proportionate way of finding that money  we are of course though all of this has  to be considered against the backdrop of  rising energy prices that  energy price cap uh coming out in what  just over a week's time when are we  going to hear more from the government  in this and your colleague james heapey  speaking on television last night said  the government is in listening more than  all of this when are we going to hear  more from the government about the  financial assistance that may be made  available as we we head into some very  very expensive times  yeah just on the national insurance  point it's worth keeping in mind that  the six and a half million people on the  lowest incomes won't pay a single extra  penny as a result of that and half of  the extra money paid will be paid by the  14 of top earners so it's just worth  keeping that in mind  on your broader question about cost of  living yes the government absolutely is  in listening mode about the general  broader cost of living issues there are  lots of measures in place already to  help address that i mean the cost of  living issue by the way is clearly a  global issue inflation in america is  higher than it is here it's higher in  germany than it is here as well it's a  global phenomenon but we do need to take  action so for example there is already a  big increase in the minimum wage planned  for april it's going up  to nine pounds fifty an hour an increase  that is well above the rate of inflation  we've already announced that the the  universal credit taper is being extended  which will put an extra thousand pounds  a year into the pockets of families on  uh lower income so all and there are a  huge number of interventions happening  on energy prices so for example uh  there's the the things like the winter  fuel allowance we've had for many years  there's also a half billion pound fund  established already to help people uh on  lower incomes with their fuel bills and  but i suspect there will be uh even more  uh done in the coming coming weeks and  months but in a targeted way to make  sure people who need some assistance  with things like fuel bills um can get  that but a lot has been done already  i wonder if we can turn our attention to  afghanistan you may well have heard us  reporting the fact that the foreign  office is is giving an additional 100  million pounds aid which i'm sure those  in afghanistan will be crying out for  but but yesterday the accusation was  made that the prime minister was  ultimately responsible for signing off  on the repatriation of of animals over  afghans he described  that allegation yesterday  as total rhubarb  what on earth did he mean by that  well i think he meant that the  allegation was not true i think that's  what he meant when he said well why  didn't you say the minister wasn't  involved in well i think he wasn't  involved in making uh individual uh  decisions and uh you know we should also  be clear by the way um that the plane  which those people and animals came out  on was not a plane that was commissioned  or chartered by the uk government it was  done privately by by donors but i think  the prime minister's made his uh  position on this very clear  it wasn't something that he was uh  personally involved in  and we should bother you  i just want i want to focus on the  language because i keep hearing that the  prime minister is gifted in its use i'm  just wondering whether or not you think  describing it as total rhubarb is is  respectful to the afghan interpreters  that we left behind some of whom are now  dead  well i think what we should be clear  about in terms of the afghan situation  is that last august in that emergency  situation the united kingdom evacuated  far more people from afghanistan than  any other european country we did it in  very short order we provided troops on  the ground to protect that air base in  kabul so i think we can be extremely  proud of what we did in afghanistan and  now we're running a program over a  five-year period that will see more  at-risk afghan civilians including or  particularly women and girls resettled  into the uk  in large 20 000 people that again that  is more than any other european country  so the fact the united kingdom has  helped and will help okay more afghans  than any other european country is  something we can be enormously proud of  yeah i understand that but despite the  fact that we have emails from the  foreign office letters from  boris johnson's then pps the prime  minister describes the allegation that  he authorized this as as total rhubarb  i'll repeat that so if the prime  minister didn't authorise it who did  well he was the prime minister wasn't  involved in making operational decisions  the plane as i said was actually  chartered privately in terms of the uh  operational decision making about who  had access to the uh the airstrip the  airfield and who got into the perimeter  of it and those would have been  operational decisions i don't know  exactly which person would have been  controlling that on a day-to-day basis  well they were working on they were  working on that together both of those  departments but let me just be clear  again the united kingdom evacuated more  people from afghanistan in august than  any other european country and we can be  extremely proud of that  i i understand that but if the prime  minister is telling us despite the  evidence that we can all see with their  own eyes where he is named in an email  from the foreign office as having  authorized this if he didn't authorize  it who did  i think it's a fairly significant  question  i've i've explained already that those  operational decisions about um on the  ground were being taken by uh teams  working in the foreign office  operationally the ministry of defense  obviously were uh in control with the  americans of kabul airport the prime  minister's been clear that it wasn't the  decision he was  involved in  i think whoever wrote that email it was  obviously a sort of second or third hand  account uh clearly it was um mistaken  and as i say the efforts we made in  afghanistan in august and that we're  making now over the next now and over  the next few years  are leading europe and that is something  we should be proud of  sure  minister i suppose the point the broader  point in all of this is that there are  legitimate concerns about the prime  minister's capacity for telling the  truth at the moment of his moral probity  in fact you yourself were openly mocked  by the audience and on bbc's any  questions uh when the host asked you if  boris johnson was a beacon of moral  integrity i mean do you stand by that do  you honestly believe mr johnson to be a  beacon of moral integrity  yeah yes i do and i said he's someone  who is working night and day and has  been since he became prime minister two  and a half years ago  to he's delivered brexit which obviously  people thought was difficult or  impossible he did that he won a  landslide general election victory uh  we've since been hit by covid and he and  his team have been working night and day  to get us through this pandemic as a  result of which we've had a world  european leading  booster programme we've got more payroll  jobs now 400 000 more than before the  pandemic youth unemployment at a record  low the imf came out this week  forecasting that we would have the  highest growth in this country of any g7  country of any g7 economy none of that  is an accident it's happened as a result  of enormous amounts of hard work that  have been led by the prime minister and  i think the results uh speak louder than  words and the results the prime minister  has delivered for this country in  extraordinarily difficult circumstances  are something that he and his team the  government and frankly all of us can be  proud of  just just finally and very very briefly  i do need to let you go um as of this  moment as a quarter past seven uh on a  friday morning the suit report has not  yet been handed to downing street is  that your understanding  uh well i spoke to someone in downing  street uh about half an hour ago and  they certainly didn't indicate that it  had been received so uh i i you know i  don't know a lot more than you do but  i've certainly got no information as of  right now that it's been received  mr thanks very much for joining us this  morning appreciate it

